Examined thread-safety distinctions between AtomicLong and Long
Good catch to question — it's not just habit, it's solving a real problem that only shows up once you remove the database.

The problem AtomicLong solves

In your old JPA setup, Oracle's @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) handled ID generation safely no matter how many requests hit the database at once — the database itself guarantees no two rows get the same ID, even under heavy concurrent writes. Spring Boot apps are multi-threaded by default (each incoming HTTP request can run on its own thread), so if two POST /accounts requests arrive at nearly the same instant, the database serializes them safely.

Now that we've removed the database, we are responsible for that guarantee. If we'd used a plain Long or long counter instead:

java
private Long idCounter = 1L;

public Account save(Account account) {
    account.setId(idCounter);
    idCounter++;   // NOT thread-safe
    store.put(account.getId(), account);
    return account;
}
idCounter++ looks like one operation but is actually three (read, increment, write). If two threads execute this at nearly the same time, both can read the same value before either writes back — and you get two accounts saved with the same ID, silently overwriting one in the map. This is a real bug, not a theoretical one, and it's the kind of thing that works fine in a single-user demo but breaks the moment two participants hit the same running instance simultaneously, or you run a quick load test.

What AtomicLong actually does

getAndIncrement() is atomic — the read-and-increment happens as one indivisible operation at the hardware/JVM level, so even under concurrent access, every thread gets a unique value with no race condition. It's part of java.util.concurrent.atomic, purpose-built for exactly this "shared counter across threads" scenario.

Worth raising in class

This is actually a nice teaching moment beyond just microservices — it's a chance to mention that an in-memory HashMap itself isn't thread-safe either (concurrent reads/writes to a HashMap can corrupt its internal structure). For a classroom demo with light traffic it'll likely never surface, but it's worth a one-line callout: "in production you'd reach for ConcurrentHashMap here instead of HashMap." I'd keep the repository code as HashMap for simplicity unless you want to make thread-safety itself a discussion point — your call depending on how deep this audience wants to go.

