Barely any change — it's a one-line swap, same method bodies. Here's Account Service's repository updated:

java
package com.unionbank.account.repository;

import com.unionbank.account.entity.Account;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

@Repository
public class AccountRepository {

    private final Map<Long, Account> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final AtomicLong idGen = new AtomicLong(1);

    public Account save(Account account) {
        account.setId(idGen.getAndIncrement());
        store.put(account.getId(), account);
        return account;
    }

    public Optional<Account> findById(Long id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id));
    }

    public List<Account> findAll() {
        return new ArrayList<>(store.values());
    }
}
Only the declaration line changed — new HashMap<>() became new ConcurrentHashMap<>(). The Map<Long, Account> reference type stays the same (that's the interface, so nothing else in the class needs to know or care which implementation backs it), and every method below it is untouched.

Apply the identical one-line change to CustomerRepository and TransactionRepository — same import added, same store declaration swapped.

This is a good one-liner to call out in class too: it's a clean example of programming to an interface — because the field type is Map, not HashMap, swapping implementations cost zero changes anywhere else in the class.